const e=[{id:"sv-5-161",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`A NOC store room is being organised with 5S. Put the five S's in the order they are applied:

- **P** — Standardize (write the rules that keep the first three S's in place)
- **Q** — Sort (remove everything not needed for current work)
- **R** — Shine (clean, and make cleaning part of everyday work)
- **S** — Sustain (audit and discipline so 5S does not unravel)
- **T** — Set in order (a labelled place for everything that remains)`,options:["Q → T → R → P → S","T → Q → R → P → S","Q → T → P → R → S","Q → R → T → P → S"],answer:0,hint:"5S order: Sort → Set in order → Shine → Standardize → Sustain (Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book numbers the steps: 1 Seiri = Sorting, 2 Seiton = Straightening (Set in order), 3 Seiso = Shining, 4 Seiketsu = Standardizing, 5 Shitsuke = Sustaining."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q (Sort) → T (Set in order) → R (Shine) → P (Standardize) → S (Sustain)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Standardize needs the first three S's to exist before it can write rules for them; Sustain is always last because it keeps the other four from unravelling."}],final:"Sort, Set in order, Shine, Standardize, Sustain → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Arranges and labels items before deciding which are needed, so unneeded items get a labelled home.","Standardize writes rules for the first three S's, so it cannot come before Shine.","Swaps Set in order and Shine; the book's step 2 is Seiton (straightening), step 3 is Seiso (shining)."],trap:"Standardize is not 'make everything look the same' at the start: it comes fourth and writes the rules for S1–S3.",bookRef:"Book pp. 503–504"},{id:"sv-5-162",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'During the **Sort** step in a regional NOC store room, the team finds about 40 items nobody can identify an owner for: old test sets, spare patch panels and boxed cables that "might be needed one day". What is the BEST action?',options:["Label every item and give each one a marked shelf location so it can be found quickly if needed","Attach a red tag to each item, move it to a holding area for a fixed period, and dispose of or redeploy anything nobody claims with a reason by the end of that period","Throw everything without a known owner away immediately so the room is cleared today","Move the items into a closed cabinet so the room looks tidy for the 5S audit"],answer:1,hint:"Sort = decide what is needed; doubtful items are red-tagged and held for a set time before a keep or dispose decision.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Sort (Seiri) eliminates everything not required for the current work. When need is uncertain, the usual method is a **red tag** with a date and a holding area, so owners can claim items before disposal."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The items have no known owner and only a vague future use, which is exactly the case red-tagging is designed for."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Labelling them is Set in order (step 2) done too early; immediate disposal skips the review; a closed cabinet only hides the clutter."}],final:"Red-tag and hold, then decide → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is Set in order applied before Sort: unneeded items get a permanent home and the clutter stays.","","Sort is a decision on need, not a purge; discarding without a review period risks losing items that are genuinely needed.","Hiding items behind doors keeps the waste and defeats the visual purpose of 5S."],bookRef:"Book p. 505"},{id:"sv-5-163",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In a 5S programme for cell-site equipment shelters, why is **Shine** described as more than cleaning?",options:["Shine means repainting and decorating the shelter so it looks professional to visiting managers","Shine is a one-time deep clean carried out just before each 5S audit","Shine is the step that writes the rules and checklists for the other S's","Cleaning is also inspection: while cleaning, technicians spot leaks, loose cables, dust on filters and other abnormal conditions early, and cleaning becomes part of everyday work"],answer:3,hint:"Seiso = Shining: clean everything, find ways to keep it clean, make cleaning part of everyday work.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines Seiso as cleaning everything and finding ways to keep it clean, making cleaning part of everyday work. A clean area makes abnormal conditions obvious."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"In a shelter, a daily or weekly clean is when a technician notices an oil stain, a loose connector or a blocked air filter — cleaning works as inspection."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Decoration, an audit-day clean and writing standards are not what Shine means."}],final:"Shine = cleaning as daily inspection → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A cosmetic view: Shine is about keeping equipment clean so abnormal conditions show up, not appearance.","The book says to make cleaning part of everyday work; an audit-day clean is exactly what 5S tries to avoid.","That describes Standardize (Seiketsu), the fourth S.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 504"},{id:"sv-5-164",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A fibre-installation depot uses **80 patch cords per day**. Replenishment takes **1.5 days**, the safety factor is **25%**, and each bin holds **25 cords**. How many kanban cards are needed?",options:["4","5","6","150"],answer:2,hint:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ — round up to a whole card.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"D = 80 per day, L = 1.5 days, S = 0.25, C = 25 per bin."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{80\\times 1.5\\times 1.25}{25}=\\dfrac{150}{25}=6$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Exactly 6 cards (no rounding needed). Fewer cards risk stock-outs during replenishment; more cards add inventory."}],final:"N = 6 cards → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Left out the lead time: 80 × 1.25 ÷ 25 = 4.","Ignored the safety factor: 80 × 1.5 ÷ 25 = 4.8, rounded up to 5.","","Forgot to divide by the container size: 80 × 1.5 × 1.25 = 150 cords, not cards."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-165",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A change-request team uses a digital kanban board. The **Review** column has a WIP limit of **4**, but it now holds **7** cards, and developers keep pulling new requests from the backlog into **In progress**. What should the team do?",options:["Raise the Review limit to 8 so that the board no longer shows a breach","Stop starting new requests and have developers help clear Review until it is back under its limit","Keep pulling new requests so that developers are never idle while reviews wait","Remove the WIP limits, because limits slow the team down"],answer:1,hint:"Kanban is a pull system: a full downstream column is a signal to stop starting and start finishing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Kanban limits inventory (here, work in progress) so that work is pulled only when downstream capacity exists. The book warns that abused kanbans backfire."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Review is over its limit, so the signal says: no new work enters; the team swarms on the bottleneck until the queue drops below 4."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Raising or removing the limit and keeping everyone busy are push behaviours that lengthen lead time."}],final:"Stop starting, start finishing → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Raising the limit to fit the queue removes the pull signal and hides the bottleneck.","","This is push behaviour: it grows WIP and lead time instead of finishing work.","WIP limits are the core of kanban; removing them returns the team to uncontrolled push."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-166",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book warns that a kanban system implemented too early **will backfire**. Which condition is a prerequisite for a successful kanban?",options:["A relatively stable demand cycle and a standard, small number of parts per card","Highly variable demand, because kanban is designed to absorb large swings","Large quantities per card, so that cards are handled less often","Defect levels can stay high, because the safety stock in each loop covers them"],answer:0,hint:"Kanban prerequisites: stable demand, standard and small quantity per card, low variation and defects, standardized work, trained handlers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's prerequisites include better changeovers, a relatively stable demand cycle, a standard (and small) number of parts per card, small variation, near-zero defects and consistent cycle times."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the option combining stable demand and a standard, small card quantity matches that list."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Variable demand, large card quantities and high defects are the conditions under which the book says kanban will fail."}],final:"Stable demand and a standard card quantity → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book lists a relatively stable demand cycle as a prerequisite; kanban is not a fix for large swings.","The book says the number of parts per card must be standard and should be as few as possible.","The book requires small variation and near-zero defects sent downstream."],trap:"Kanban is a building block on top of 5S, standard work and kaizen; it is not a quick fix for large inventory.",bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-167",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'When an agent enters an unverified address, the order system shows a pop-up: *"Address not validated — continue?"*. Agents usually click **Continue**, and failed installations caused by bad addresses have not fallen. What is the BEST improvement?',options:["Retrain agents to read pop-up messages carefully before clicking","Make the pop-up red and add a sound so that it is harder to ignore","Have the back office inspect 100% of orders the next morning","Change the warning into a control that blocks the order until the address is matched against the address database"],answer:3,hint:"A warning can be overridden; a control-type poka-yoke stops the error from going forward. Type 1 prevents, Type 2 detects and stops, Type 3 inspects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book ranks controls: Type 1 prevention (the error cannot happen) and Type 2 detection that stops the process are the strongest. A pop-up that can be dismissed is a weak warning."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Blocking the order until the address is validated turns the warning into a control: an unvalidated order cannot move forward."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Training and louder warnings still depend on attention; next-day inspection only contains the defect."}],final:"Turn the warning into a blocking control → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Still relies on human attention, which is why the warning is being ignored.","A louder warning is still a warning the agent can override.","Inspection is a Type 3 control (containment); it finds errors after they are made and is not sustainable.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-168",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"After each fusion splice, the splicing machine estimates the splice loss. If the loss is above **0.1 dB**, the machine **locks out the next step**, so the bad splice cannot be closed into the enclosure until it is redone. How does the book classify this control?",options:["Type 1 corrective action — prevention, because the defect can never happen","Type 2 corrective action — detection that stops the process so the defect cannot move forward","Type 3 corrective action — inspection used as short-term containment","SPC on the X's with trained operators"],answer:1,hint:"Type 1 = error cannot occur; Type 2 = error detected and process stopped; Type 3 = inspection or containment.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: Type 1 eliminates the error condition (prevention poka-yoke). Type 2 detects that an error has occurred and stops the process so the defect does not move forward (detection poka-yoke)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A high-loss splice can still be made; the machine detects it and blocks the next step. That is detection plus stop → Type 2."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Not Type 1 (the defect happens), not Type 3 (no human inspection or audit), not SPC (no chart)."}],final:"Detect and stop → Type 2 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The bad splice has already been made; the device detects it. Type 1 would stop the error condition from occurring at all.","","Type 3 is an audit or 100% inspection that is likely to find the defect; it does not lock the process.","No control chart is involved; the machine itself checks each splice and stops the next step."],trap:"'The defect cannot reach the customer' is not the same as 'the defect cannot happen'. Only the second is Type 1.",bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-169",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are **prevention (Type 1)** mistake-proofing devices EXCEPT:",options:["A dashboard alarm that flags an order after it has been submitted with the wrong tariff","A SIM tray shaped so the card fits only one way round","A drop-down list that offers only the plans valid for the customer's handset","A keyed connector that cannot be plugged into the wrong port"],answer:0,hint:"Prevention removes the chance of the error; detection finds it after it has happened.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type 1 (prevention) poka-yoke makes the error impossible. A device that reacts after the error is detection (Type 2 if it stops the process; otherwise only a warning)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The SIM tray, drop-down list and keyed connector all make the wrong action impossible. The dashboard alarm only fires after a wrong order has been submitted."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The odd one out is the after-the-fact alarm."}],final:"The after-submission alarm is detection, not prevention → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Shape-based design makes the wrong orientation impossible: a classic prevention device.","Invalid plans cannot be chosen, so the error cannot occur: prevention.","The wrong connection is physically impossible: prevention."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-170",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt is setting up a new chart (Phase I) for provisioning time.

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Provisioning Time (h)    n = 5, 25 subgroups

Test Results for R Chart
  No tests failed.

Test Results for Xbar Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  7
\`\`\`

The log shows that subgroup 7 was collected on the day of a **ticketing-tool outage**. The fault has been fixed and a fail-over has been added so it cannot recur. What should the Black Belt do before using the limits for ongoing control?`,options:["Exclude subgroup 7, record the reason, and recalculate the centre line and limits from the remaining subgroups","Keep subgroup 7, because all collected data must be used when calculating limits","Widen the limits to 4 standard deviations so that point 7 falls inside","Conclude the process is not capable and restart the improvement project"],answer:0,hint:"Phase I: points with an identified and removed special cause are excluded, then limits are recalculated from common-cause data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Control limits should describe common-cause variation only. In Phase I, a signal with a found and corrected special cause is removed before the limits are frozen."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Point 7 has a known, documented cause that cannot recur, and the R chart is in control. Drop subgroup 7, note why, and recalculate."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Keeping the point inflates the limits; widening limits hides signals; capability is a different question."}],final:"Remove the explained point and recalculate → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Keeping a point with a known, removed special cause inflates the limits and makes the chart less sensitive.","Moving the limits to fit the data hides signals; limits are set at 3σ from common-cause data.","A single explained special cause says nothing about capability; capability is judged against specifications."],trap:"Only remove points whose special cause is found and fixed. Deleting unexplained points just to make the chart look clean is not allowed.",bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529, 553"},{id:"sv-5-171",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Activation Time (min)    n = 5

Test Results for R Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  4, 11, 19

Test Results for Xbar Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  11
\`\`\`

What is the correct next step?`,options:["Investigate only Xbar point 11, because customers feel the average, not the range","Investigate the R-chart signals first, because the Xbar limits are calculated from R-bar and cannot be trusted until the R chart is in control","Switch to an Xbar-S chart so the R-chart signals disappear","Ignore the R chart, because range signals with n = 5 are usually false alarms"],answer:1,hint:"Read the range (or S, or MR) chart first: the location chart's limits are built from its average.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Xbar limits are $\\bar{\\bar{x}}\\pm A_2\\bar{R}$, so they depend on R-bar. If within-subgroup variation is unstable, R-bar — and the Xbar limits — are wrong."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The R chart signals at 4, 11 and 19. Find and remove those causes first; then judge the Xbar chart (point 11 may share the same cause)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Looking only at Xbar, changing the chart type or dismissing R signals all skip the required order."}],final:"R chart first, then Xbar → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Ignores the R chart; with within-subgroup variation out of control, R-bar and therefore the Xbar limits are unreliable.","","Changing chart type does not remove special causes; the S chart would flag the same unstable spread.","There is no such rule; three R-chart signals in 25 subgroups is strong evidence of unstable spread."],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 538"},{id:"sv-5-172",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team charts repair time before and after its improvement, using Minitab's **Stage** option:

\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Repair Time (h) by Stage

Stage     Mean     UCL      LCL     Tests failed
Before    6.80    10.40     3.20     none
After     4.10     5.60     2.60     none (30 points)
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["Nothing has changed, because neither stage has any failed tests","The After stage is less capable, because its control limits are closer together","Keep monitoring with the Before limits so the improvement does not trigger false signals","Both the mean and the spread fell, the After stage is stable, and the After limits should now be used for ongoing control"],answer:3,hint:"After a known improvement, recalculate the centre line and limits from the new, stable data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: a reduction in variation or a known process shift should lead to recalculating the centre line and control limits."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The mean fell from 6.80 h to 4.10 h, and the limit width fell from 7.20 h to 3.00 h. The After stage has 30 points with no failed tests, so it is stable and its limits become the control limits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"'No failures' within each stage does not mean 'no change'; narrower limits are good; old limits are too wide to catch a slide back."}],final:"Improvement confirmed → use the After limits → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Each stage is judged against its own limits; comparing the stages shows a clear drop in mean and width.","Narrower limits mean less common-cause variation, which is better, not worse.","The wide Before limits (3.20–10.40) would let the process drift back almost to its old level without a signal.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 553"},{id:"sv-5-173",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Provisioning time is known to change between the **morning and evening shifts**. The team takes a subgroup of **five orders once a day at 10:00** for its Xbar-R chart. What is the main weakness of this plan?",options:["The subgroup is too small; taking 20 orders at 10:00 would fix the problem","Provisioning time should be charted on a p chart instead","Samples must be taken at least as often as the process can change; sampling only at 10:00 never sees the evening shift","Daily sampling is too frequent; weekly sampling would be more economical"],answer:2,hint:"Sampling frequency must match how often the process can change, so every source of change (shift, team, day) gets a chance to show.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Rational subgrouping and sampling frequency should let the chart see each way the process can change, such as shifts, crews or days."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A shift-to-shift change can only show up if both shifts are sampled, for example one subgroup per shift or every few hours."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Larger subgroups, a different chart type or less frequent sampling do not cover the evening shift."}],final:"Sample both shifts — frequency must match how the process changes → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A bigger subgroup at the same time of day still never sees the evening shift.","Time is continuous data; a p chart is for proportion defective.","","Less frequent sampling makes the blind spot worse."],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-174",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A stable process is monitored with 3-sigma control limits and only Test 1 (one point beyond 3σ). About how often will a point fall outside the limits **even though nothing has changed**?",options:["About 5 points in 100 (5%)","About 32 points in 100","About 3 points in 1,000 (roughly 0.3%)","Never — a stable process cannot produce a point outside 3σ limits"],answer:2,hint:"±3σ covers about 99.7% of a normal distribution; about 0.3% falls outside.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: control limits represent 99.73% of natural variability for normal distributions, so there is only about a 0.3% chance that a point from an unchanged process falls outside."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"0.3% is about 3 points in 1,000. This is the chart's false-alarm (Type I) risk for Test 1."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"5% belongs to a 95% interval; 32% to ±1σ; 'never' ignores the tails."}],final:"≈ 0.3% false alarms → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Confuses the chart with a hypothesis test at α = 0.05; ±3σ covers 99.73%, not 95%.","That is the share outside ±1σ, not ±3σ.","","Limits cover 99.73% of a normal distribution, so false alarms are rare but possible."],trap:"Adding more tests (2, 3, 5, 6 …) increases the overall false-alarm rate above 0.3%.",bookRef:"Book pp. 525, 527"},{id:"sv-5-175",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are good reasons to choose an **I-MR** chart EXCEPT:",options:["Only one value is produced per period, such as a daily churn rate","Each measurement is expensive or slow, so forming subgroups is not practical","A homogeneous batch where one reading represents the whole batch","Five calls can easily be sampled every hour, and the team wants the most sensitive chart for small shifts in the mean"],answer:3,hint:"I-MR: low volume, long cycle, homogeneous batch or costly sampling. When subgroups are easy, Xbar-R or Xbar-S is more sensitive.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's selection table: I-MR when production is low volume, cycle time is long, a homogeneous sample represents the whole batch, or sampling is costly; I-MR limits are wider than Xbar limits."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Hourly subgroups of five calls are easy to collect, and the goal is sensitivity to mean shifts. That points to Xbar-R, not I-MR."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other three are all reasons the book gives for using I-MR."}],final:"Easy subgroups → use Xbar-R, not I-MR → answer **D**",whyWrong:["One value per period is the classic reason for an individuals chart.","The book lists costly sampling as a reason to avoid subgroups and use I-MR.","The book lists a homogeneous sample representing the whole batch as an I-MR case.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 531"},{id:"sv-5-176",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt chooses an attribute control chart for a new KPI. Put the decisions in the correct order:

- **P** — Decide whether the data are continuous or attribute
- **Q** — Decide whether you are counting defects or defective units
- **R** — Check whether the sample size (area of opportunity) is constant
- **S** — Select the chart: np, p, c or u`,options:["P → Q → R → S","Q → P → R → S","R → P → Q → S","P → R → S → Q"],answer:0,hint:"Data type → defects or defectives → constant or varying sample size → chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Chart selection is a decision tree: data type first, then for attribute data defects (c or u) vs defectives (np or p), then constant vs varying sample size."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"P (data type) → Q (defects or defectives) → R (constant n?) → S (pick np, p, c or u)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Any order that picks the chart before the last question, or asks about n first, skips a branch."}],final:"Data type, count type, sample size, chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Defects vs defectives is only a question once you know the data are attribute.","Sample-size constancy only matters after the data type and count type are known.","The chart cannot be selected before knowing whether defects or defectives are counted."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-177",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A team proposes these charts for four KPIs:

| Row | KPI | Data collected | Proposed chart |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Call set-up time | 5 calls sampled every hour | Xbar-R |
| 2 | Failed port requests | Number failed out of a daily volume that varies | p |
| 3 | Fibre faults | Faults found per inspection; the km of route inspected varies each week | c |
| 4 | Data-hall energy use | One meter reading per day | I-MR |

Which row pairs the KPI with the WRONG chart?`,options:["Row 1","Row 2","Row 3","Row 4"],answer:2,hint:"Defects with a constant area of opportunity → c; defects with a varying area → u.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"c and u charts both count defects. The c chart needs a constant area of opportunity; the u chart plots defects per unit and allows it to vary."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"Row 3 counts faults (defects) over a varying number of km, so it should be a **u chart** (faults per km)."},{title:"Check the others",body:"Row 1 → Xbar-R ✓; Row 2 → p ✓; Row 4 → I-MR ✓."}],final:"Row 3 should be a u chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Continuous data in hourly subgroups of 5 → Xbar-R is correct.","Defective units with a varying sample size → p chart is correct.","","One continuous value per day → I-MR is correct."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 531"},{id:"sv-5-178",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Daily Port-In Lead Time (h)

Test Results for I Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  14

Test Results for MR Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  14, 15
\`\`\`

Which interpretation is correct?`,options:["Three separate special causes occurred, one for each failed point","Most likely one unusual day: the moving ranges at 14 and 15 both include day 14's value, so the investigation should focus on day 14","The process variation increased permanently from day 14 onward","Day 15 is the special cause, because the last MR signal is at point 15"],answer:1,hint:"$MR_i=|x_i-x_{i-1}|$, so one extreme value appears in two consecutive moving ranges.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A moving range is $MR_i=|x_i-x_{i-1}|$. One extreme value $x_{14}$ makes both $MR_{14}=|x_{14}-x_{13}|$ and $MR_{15}=|x_{15}-x_{14}|$ large."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The I chart flags only day 14, and the MR chart flags 14 and 15. Together these show one spike on day 14."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Counting three causes, reading a lasting change or blaming day 15 all misread how moving ranges are built."}],final:"One event on day 14 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Each moving range uses two consecutive values, so one extreme value inflates two MR points.","","Only two MR points signal, then the chart returns to normal; that is a single spike, not a lasting change.","MR15 is large only because it is measured from the extreme day-14 value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–538"},{id:"sv-5-179",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:'A dispatch team records, for each day, the **number of minutes by which the first technician arrived late** (one value per day). A colleague proposes a **c chart** "because lateness is a defect". Which chart is MOST appropriate?',options:["I-MR chart","c chart","p chart","Xbar-R chart"],answer:0,hint:"Continuous data, one value per period → I-MR. Attribute charts are only for counts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The data type decides the chart. Minutes are continuous (measured) data. A c chart counts defects in a constant area of opportunity."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"One continuous value per day → individuals and moving range chart."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"c and p are attribute charts; Xbar-R needs more than one value per subgroup."}],final:"Continuous, one per day → I-MR → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Minutes late is a continuous measurement, not a count of defects.","There is no proportion of defective units here; each day gives one measured value.","Xbar-R needs subgroups; here there is only one value per day."],trap:"The word 'defect' in a stem does not make the data attribute data; check what is actually recorded.",bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-180",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An Xbar-R chart of **router-boot time** uses subgroups of **n = 5** and has $\\bar{R}=4.0$ s. Constants: n = 4: D₃ = 0, D₄ = 2.282; n = 5: A₂ = 0.577, D₃ = 0, D₄ = 2.114. What is the **UCL of the R chart**?",options:["0 s","6.31 s","8.46 s","9.13 s"],answer:2,hint:"$$UCL_R=D_4\\bar{R}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 5, $\\bar{R}=4.0$ s, D₄ = 2.114 for n = 5."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL_R=D_4\\times\\bar{R}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$UCL_R=2.114\\times 4.0=8.456\\approx 8.46$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Any subgroup range above 8.46 s signals unusual within-subgroup variation."}],final:"UCL_R = 8.46 s → answer **C**",whyWrong:["That is the LCL: D₃ × R-bar = 0 × 4.0.","Added A₂ × R-bar to R-bar (Xbar-chart constant used on the R chart).","","Used the n = 4 constant D₄ = 2.282 instead of n = 5."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-181",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On an Xbar-R chart, what do the two charts monitor?",options:["The R chart monitors changes in the mean between subgroups; the Xbar chart monitors the spread within subgroups","The R chart monitors the distance between the specification limits; the Xbar chart monitors individual units against the specification","The R chart monitors within-subgroup (short-term) variation; the Xbar chart monitors changes in process location between subgroups","Both charts monitor the same thing; the R chart is only a back-up in case the Xbar chart fails"],answer:2,hint:"Range, MR and S charts monitor within variation; Xbar and I charts monitor location.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: Range, Moving Range or Standard Deviation charts are used to monitor within variation. The Xbar chart plots subgroup averages to show shifts in location."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"R = spread inside each subgroup; Xbar = where the process is centred from subgroup to subgroup."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Swapped roles, specification limits and 'back-up' are all wrong."}],final:"R = within; Xbar = location between subgroups → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The roles are reversed.","Control charts use control limits from process data, not specification limits.","","They monitor different components of variation: spread and location."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532, 538"},{id:"sv-5-182",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A c chart counts **alarms per week** on a fixed cluster of 40 cell sites. Over the baseline, $\\bar{c}=16$. What are the **control limits**?",options:["LCL = 12, UCL = 20","LCL = 4, UCL = 28","LCL = 0, UCL = 64","LCL = 5.36, UCL = 26.64"],answer:1,hint:"$$\\bar{c}\\pm 3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}$$ (a negative LCL is set to 0)",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{c}=16$ alarms per week; the area of opportunity (40 sites) is constant."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL,LCL=\\bar{c}\\pm 3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}=3\\times\\sqrt{16}=3\\times 4=12$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$UCL=16+12=28 \\qquad LCL=16-12=4$$"}],final:"LCL = 4, UCL = 28 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used 1 standard deviation (±4) instead of 3.","","Used 3 × c-bar instead of 3 × √c-bar (the negative LCL of −32 was then set to 0).","Used the I-chart constant 2.66 instead of 3."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 541"},{id:"sv-5-183",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A c chart assumes the area of opportunity (the size of the inspected unit) is the same for every point. If it varies, a u chart should be used instead. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"c = defects per constant unit; u = defects per unit when the unit size varies.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the u chart and c chart both track defects, but the u chart does not need a constant sample size as the c chart does, and its limits vary with the sample size."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"Varying area of opportunity → convert counts to defects per unit → u chart. The statement is true."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book says the u chart does not need a constant sample size, as the c chart does. So the statement is true."],bookRef:"Book p. 520"},{id:"sv-5-184",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
P Chart of Failed Fibre Activations
P-bar = 0.0800
Tests performed with unequal sample sizes

Test Results for P Chart
  No tests failed (25 days).
\`\`\`

The business target is a failure rate of **3%**. Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["The process is in control, so its performance is acceptable and no action is needed","Investigate the day with the highest proportion as a special cause","Recalculate the limits around 0.03 so that the bad days start to signal","The process is stable but is not meeting the target; reacting to individual days would be tampering, so a fundamental change to the process is needed"],answer:3,hint:"Stable ≠ meeting requirements. Common-cause problems need a system change, not reaction to single points.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A chart with no failed tests shows only common-cause variation: the process is predictable. Whether it meets the business need is a separate question."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The process predictably fails about 8% of activations against a 3% target. Point-by-point reaction will not help; the system itself must change (for example a new improvement project)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"'Acceptable', 'chase the worst day' and 'limits from the target' are the classic common-cause mistakes."}],final:"Stable but off target → change the system → answer **D**",whyWrong:["In control means predictable, not good: 8% is predictably above the 3% target.","With no tests failed, every day is common-cause variation; chasing the highest day is tampering.","Control limits come from the voice of the process; setting them from a target mixes the voice of the customer into the chart.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539"},{id:"sv-5-185",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Each week a survey asks customers whether they were dissatisfied with their installation (yes or no). The number of respondents varies between **180 and 420** a week. Which chart should track the dissatisfaction rate?",options:["np chart","p chart","u chart","c chart"],answer:1,hint:"Defectives (yes or no per unit) with a varying sample size → p chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each respondent is classified as dissatisfied or not, so the data are defectives. With a varying sample size, plot the proportion: p chart."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Weekly n varies between 180 and 420 → p chart, with limits that vary with n."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"np needs a constant n; c and u are for defect counts."}],final:"Defectives, varying n → p chart → answer **B**",whyWrong:["An np chart needs a constant sample size; here it varies from 180 to 420.","","A u chart counts defects per unit; each respondent is a yes or no (defective) unit.","A c chart counts defects in a constant area of opportunity."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-186",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
NP Chart of Defective Installation Records    n = 300
NP-bar = 15.00   UCL = 26.32   LCL = 3.68

Test Results for NP Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  23   (value = 1)
\`\`\`

What is the BEST first step?`,options:["Verify the measurement for day 23 (whether all 300 records were checked, a new auditor, or a changed checklist), then, if it is genuine, find what went right and make it standard","Ignore it, because a low number of defectives is good news","Recalculate the limits at once, including day 23","Stop the installation process until the cause is found"],answer:0,hint:"A point below the LCL is a special cause: check the measurement first, then learn from it if it is real.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Signals below the LCL on defect or defective charts are special causes too. They often come from measurement or inspection problems (a skipped sample, a new auditor). If real, they show how to improve."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"1 defective against an expected 15 (LCL 3.68). Confirm that the full sample was checked with the normal checklist before celebrating."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring the signal, recalculating at once and stopping the process all skip verification."}],final:"Verify, then learn → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A signal below the LCL is still a special cause; it may be a real gain to keep or a measurement failure.","One point is not a sustained shift; first confirm that the signal is real and understand it.","Nothing indicates harm to customers; stopping is not the first response to a low-side signal."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 540"},{id:"sv-5-187",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A speech-analytics tool automatically extracts the handle time of **15 calls every hour**. The team wants a chart of hourly average handle time and its spread. Which chart is MOST appropriate?",options:["Xbar-R chart","I-MR chart","Xbar-S chart","p chart"],answer:2,hint:"Continuous data in larger subgroups (roughly n > 8–10), or when outliers distort R → Xbar-S.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: Xbar and S charts are used instead of R charts when there are concerns about the range, which is sensitive to outliers and wastes data in large subgroups."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Subgroups of 15 continuous values every hour → Xbar-S."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Xbar-R suits small subgroups; I-MR suits one value at a time; p is attribute."}],final:"n = 15 → Xbar-S → answer **C**",whyWrong:["With large subgroups the range uses only the two extreme values and wastes information; S is preferred.","Charting individuals ignores the natural hourly subgroup of 15 and loses sensitivity.","","Handle time is continuous data, not a proportion defective."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-188",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is plotted on a **CuSum** chart?",options:["A weighted average of the current value and all previous values, with more weight on recent data","The absolute difference between consecutive values","A running count of units outside the specification limits","The cumulative sum of the deviations of each value (or subgroup mean) from the target"],answer:3,hint:"CuSum: $C_i=\\sum (x_j-T)$ — every past deviation counts equally.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"CuSum adds up each point's deviation from the target. A small, sustained shift makes the sum grow steadily, so CuSum detects small shifts that a Shewhart chart misses."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The book: same reasons as EWMA, except that past data are as important as present data — every deviation stays in the sum with equal weight."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Weighted averages are EWMA; consecutive differences are MR; specification counts are not SPC."}],final:"Cumulative sum of deviations from target → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This describes an EWMA chart.","This is the moving range (MR) chart.","CuSum does not use specification limits; it sums deviations from a target.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 531"},{id:"sv-5-189",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"An EWMA chart plots $z_i=\\lambda x_i+(1-\\lambda)z_{i-1}$. If the weighting factor is set to **λ = 1**, what does the EWMA chart become?",options:["A CuSum chart, because all past data are weighted equally","A chart with very long memory, ideal for detecting tiny shifts","A Shewhart chart of the individual values (or subgroup means), because all the weight is on the current point","A chart that can never signal, because the control limits collapse to zero"],answer:2,hint:"Small λ = long memory, sensitive to small shifts; λ = 1 = no memory = Shewhart chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With λ = 1: $z_i=1\\times x_i+0\\times z_{i-1}=x_i$. The plotted value is the current point only."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"With no smoothing, the EWMA chart is the same as an individuals (or Xbar) Shewhart chart, which is good for large shifts and poor for small ones."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Equal weighting of the past is CuSum; long memory needs a small λ; the limits do not vanish."}],final:"λ = 1 → Shewhart chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["λ = 1 gives no weight to past data, the opposite of CuSum.","Long memory comes from a small λ, not λ = 1.","","With λ = 1 the limits become ordinary 3σ Shewhart limits; they do not collapse."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-190",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Xbar Chart of Average Handle Time (s)

TEST 7. 15 points within 1 standard deviation of center line
        (above and below CL).
Test Failed at points:  41
\`\`\`

What does this signal MOST likely mean?`,options:["The process mean is drifting upward","It is a false alarm, because no point is outside the control limits","The process is producing output outside the specification limits","Variation has fallen sharply compared with the limits; find the cause so the improvement can be sustained, and consider recalculating the limits"],answer:3,hint:"Test 7 = 15 in a row in Zone C: unusually small variation (or limits that are too wide, or mixed streams in the subgroups).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: Test 7 (15 points in a row within one sigma) indicates a dramatic improvement in variation. You still want to find the special cause so the improvement can be sustained."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Hugging the centre line means the process is now much less variable than when the limits were set. Find out why, then recalculate the limits. If subgroups mix several streams, the limits may also be too wide."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Not a drift, not a false alarm, and not a specification issue."}],final:"Test 7 = reduced variation → find and keep the cause → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A drift shows as a trend (Test 3) or a run on one side (Test 2); Test 7 points sit on both sides of the CL.","Non-random patterns inside the limits are special causes too; that is why Tests 2–8 exist.","Control tests compare points with control limits, not specifications.",""],trap:"Test 7 is a 'good news' signal, but it is still a special cause that needs investigation.",bookRef:"Book p. 537"},{id:"sv-5-191",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A p chart of each of 30 billing agents' **weekly error rate** shows **all agents within the control limits**. The manager plans to give a performance warning every week to the **three agents with the highest rates**. What is the BEST response?",options:["Agree: someone is always worst, and warnings will push everyone to improve","All agents are within common-cause variation, so the differences come from the system; improve the system (methods, tools, training) and investigate only agents outside the limits","Warn the bottom three and reward the top three to balance motivation","Recalculate the limits every week so that the bottom three fall outside them"],answer:1,hint:"Common cause → act on the system. Special cause → act on the specific point or person.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Points inside the limits with no failed tests are common-cause variation. Treating them as special causes (blaming individuals, adjusting after each point) is tampering and usually makes things worse."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The bottom three will change from week to week by chance. Lasting gains come from changing the process every agent works in."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Warnings, rewards and manipulated limits all react to noise."}],final:"Fix the system, not the people → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Ranking within common-cause variation punishes people for random variation in the system — a form of tampering.","","Rewarding and punishing random variation are both reactions to common cause.","Moving limits to create signals defeats the purpose of SPC."],bookRef:"Book pp. 516, 532"},{id:"sv-5-192",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
C Chart of Weekly Security Incidents
C-bar = 2.40   UCL = 7.05   LCL = 0

Test Results for C Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  17
\`\`\`

Weeks 9 and 12 each had **zero** incidents. Which statement is correct?`,options:["Week 17 is above the UCL and is a special cause; with LCL = 0, a zero week cannot fail Test 1, so an improvement could only show through a run test such as Test 2","Weeks 9 and 12 are special causes below the LCL and should be investigated","Week 17 is inside the limits, because the UCL of a c chart is c-bar + 3 = 5.4","Tests 5–8 should be switched on to check weeks 9 and 12 for zone patterns"],answer:0,hint:"$$UCL=\\bar{c}+3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}$$; a negative LCL is set to 0, so no point can fall below it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"$$LCL=\\bar{c}-3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}=2.40-3\\times 1.549=-2.25\\rightarrow 0$$ A count of zero equals the LCL and cannot be beyond it."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Week 17 failed Test 1 on the high side: investigate it. Low counts can only be detected by run tests (for example 9 in a row below the CL), which are valid for attribute data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Zero weeks are not beyond the limits; the UCL formula uses √c-bar; zone tests 5–8 are for variables charts only."}],final:"Week 17 is a signal; zero weeks cannot fail Test 1 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A count cannot be below 0, so with LCL = 0 a zero week is inside the limits.","The UCL is c-bar + 3√c-bar = 7.05, as Minitab prints.","The book says Tests 5–8 apply only to variables data, not to attribute charts."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532, 541"},{id:"sv-5-193",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"If a control chart shows no failed tests, the process is producing output within its specification limits. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Control limits = voice of the process; specification limits = voice of the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A chart with no signals shows a stable, predictable process. Whether that process is inside the specification is a capability question (Cp, Cpk)."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"A process can be perfectly in control while predictably producing defects. The statement is false."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Control limits describe how the process behaves, not what the customer requires; a stable process can still be outside the specification.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 516, 525"},{id:"sv-5-194",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A new automated fraud check costs **OMR 36,000** once. It saves **OMR 4,500 per month** in chargebacks but adds **OMR 1,500 per month** in licence fees. What is the simple payback period?",options:["6 months","8 months","8.3 months","12 months"],answer:3,hint:"$$\\text{Payback}=\\dfrac{\\text{one-time cost}}{\\text{net saving per period}}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"One-time cost OMR 36,000; gross saving OMR 4,500 per month; running cost OMR 1,500 per month."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Payback}=\\dfrac{\\text{one-time cost}}{\\text{saving}-\\text{running cost}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Payback}=\\dfrac{36000}{4500-1500}=\\dfrac{36000}{3000}=12\\ \\text{months}$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"The control pays for itself after one year. Running costs must always be netted off before comparing options."}],final:"Payback = 12 months → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Added the licence fee to the saving: 36,000 ÷ 6,000.","Ignored the monthly licence fee: 36,000 ÷ 4,500.","Added one month's fee to the one-time cost: 37,500 ÷ 4,500.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 557–561"},{id:"sv-5-195",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt claims three benefits: **(i)** overtime spend fell by OMR 40,000 and the overtime budget was cut; **(ii)** the three extra agents planned in next year's budget will no longer be hired; **(iii)** agents freed 600 hours a year, which are now used for other tasks. How should Finance classify them?",options:["(i) hard saving; (ii) cost avoidance; (iii) soft saving","All three are hard savings, because each reduces cost","(i) soft saving; (ii) hard saving; (iii) hard saving","(i) hard saving; (ii) hard saving; (iii) not a benefit at all"],answer:0,hint:"Hard = reduces actual spend or budget; cost avoidance = prevents a future cost; soft = capacity or productivity gains with no cash reduction.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Finance (the financial representative who validates benefits) separates savings that show in the P&L from benefits that do not."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"(i) the budget line itself is reduced → hard. (ii) a planned future cost will not be spent → cost avoidance. (iii) time is freed but no spend falls → soft."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Calling everything hard overstates the project; ignoring soft benefits understates it."}],final:"Hard, avoidance, soft → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Only (i) reduces an existing budget line; (ii) avoids a future cost and (iii) frees capacity without reducing spend.","Reversed: a cut to an existing budget is hard; freed hours with no spend reduction are soft.","Avoiding planned hires is cost avoidance, not a hard saving; freed capacity is a real but soft benefit."],bookRef:"Book pp. 557–558, 585"},{id:"sv-5-196",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`Six months after handoff, the MTTR measure in the control plan moves to a **new ticketing tool** that timestamps "restored" automatically instead of relying on the agent's entry. The I chart immediately fails **Test 2 below** the centre line. What should the process owner do FIRST?`,options:["Report the drop as a process improvement and reward the team","Ignore the signal, because it is caused only by the new tool","Treat the measurement change as the likely cause: verify the new measurement system (and compare it with the old method), update the control plan's measurement method and, if confirmed, recalculate the limits","Return to manual timestamps so the chart matches the old limits"],answer:2,hint:"Any change to how a KPI is measured requires the measurement system to be validated and the control plan and limits to be updated.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A control plan names the measurement method for each characteristic. If the measurement system changes, the data can shift even though the process has not."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The signal started on the day the tool changed. Validate the new system (MSA, side-by-side comparison), document the new method in the control plan, and recalculate the limits on the new basis."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Celebrating, ignoring and going back to the old tool all skip the validation step."}],final:"Validate the new measurement, update the plan, recalculate the limits → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The process did not change; only the measurement did. Celebrating could hide a definition change.","A changed measurement system invalidates the current limits; it must be validated and the plan updated, not ignored.","","Going back to a less reliable method to protect old limits is the wrong way round."],bookRef:"Book pp. 562–570"},{id:"sv-5-197",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A control plan for installation quality lists the output (Y = failed installations) **and** the critical inputs (Xs, such as address validation and appointment confirmation). Why monitor the Xs?",options:["Because Xs are easier to explain to customers than the output","Because monitoring the Xs removes the need to measure the Y","Because the Xs must be listed only as a record of what was analysed in the Analyze phase","Because Y = f(X): controlling the critical Xs keeps the process right before the Y moves, giving earlier and more effective control"],answer:3,hint:"Control the vital few Xs upstream; monitor the Y to confirm.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The vital few Xs drive the Y. The book ranks SPC on the X's (with monitoring of the Y's) above inspection and SOPs, because problems are caught at their source."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If address validation or appointment confirmation slips, failed installations will follow. Watching those Xs gives warning before customers are affected."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Customer reporting, dropping the Y and 'just a record' miss the point of controlling inputs."}],final:"Control Xs to keep Y on target → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Customer reporting is about the Y; Xs are monitored to prevent problems.","The book's ranking is SPC on the X's with appropriate monitoring on the Y's; the Y is still checked.","The control plan is a working document for running the process, not a project record.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 562"},{id:"sv-5-198",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are typical columns of a process **control plan** EXCEPT:",options:["Measurement method, sample size and frequency","The project's estimated annual financial savings","Control method (for example the chart type or the poka-yoke used)","Reaction plan, with the person responsible"],answer:1,hint:"Control-plan columns: step, characteristic or KPI, specification or target, measurement method, sample size and frequency, control method, owner, reaction plan.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A control plan tells the process owner what to measure, how, how often, how it is controlled, who owns it and how to react."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Measurement, control method and reaction plan with an owner are all standard columns. Estimated savings belong to the project's cost-benefit documentation, validated by Finance."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The savings estimate is the odd one out."}],final:"Savings figures are not a control-plan column → answer **B**",whyWrong:["These tell the owner how and how often each characteristic is checked: core control-plan columns.","","The control method is a core column.","Every row links to what to do and who does it when a signal or failure occurs."],bookRef:"Book pp. 562–566"},{id:"sv-5-199",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'An Xbar chart of billing-run accuracy signals during the overnight run. The operator suspects a corrupted rate table, but the response plan says only *"notify the billing manager"*. The manager cannot be reached, the run continues, and 80,000 wrong bills are sent. What was MISSING from the response plan?',options:["Defined containment actions and the authority to hold or stop the run, with an escalation path and a named deputy","More frequent sampling during the overnight run","Wider control limits to reduce false alarms at night","A monthly review meeting to discuss signals"],answer:0,hint:"Response plan = trigger, recommended response, responsibilities (who can act), documentation, and the conditions to record.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's response-plan components include the triggers, the recommended response and the responsibilities for responding. It also stresses that management must empower people to act when a chart signals."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The operator had a valid signal but no defined action and no authority to stop the run, and the only contact was unavailable. The plan needed containment steps, stop authority and an escalation chain."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Detection worked, so more sampling or wider limits do not address the failure; a monthly meeting is too slow."}],final:"Containment and stop authority were missing → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The chart did signal in time; the failure was in the reaction, not the detection.","The signal was real; wider limits would have hidden it.","A monthly review cannot stop a run that is going wrong tonight."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 571"},{id:"sv-5-200",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"On an I chart of daily port-in lead time, today's point is **inside the control limits**, but the value is **outside the customer specification**. What should the response plan require?",options:["No action, because the chart shows the process is in control","Treat the point as a special cause and adjust the process at once","Recalculate the control limits so that they equal the specification limits","Handle it as nonconforming output (contain and fix the affected orders and customers), and recognise that a stable process producing out-of-spec results has a capability problem"],answer:3,hint:"Out of spec → product reaction (containment). Out of control → process reaction (find the special cause). They are different triggers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The response plan is triggered by defects as well as by out-of-control points. Specification limits and control limits answer different questions."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The affected orders must be contained and fixed now. Because the process is stable, the out-of-spec result comes from common-cause variation, so it needs a capability improvement, not a point-by-point adjustment."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Doing nothing ignores the customer; adjusting is tampering; specification-based limits mix the voice of the customer into the chart."}],final:"Contain the output; address capability → answer **D**",whyWrong:["In control is not the same as within specification; the affected customers still need containment.","The chart shows no special cause; adjusting for a common-cause point is tampering.","Control limits come from process data; replacing them with specification limits destroys the chart's purpose.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 571–572"}];export{e as items};
